# Principal-Based RBAC (Principal → Role → Permission → Resource) — security-access-control

## Context

This spec (final version, iterated across this conversation) specifies a production RBAC model built around a generic `Principal` identity (human user, mobile app, web app, AI agent, service account, external system, microservice) instead of the previous `security-access-control` service's fixed `User`/`UserRole` enum. Per the user's explicit decision: **`Principal` fully replaces `User`** — `User`, `UserRole`, and everything built against them (`AuthServiceImpl`, `UserDetailsServiceImpl`, `JwtTokenProvider`, `AuthController`, `IUserRepository`, `IUserMapper`, `UserDTO`) were rewired to `Principal`, not kept alongside it. There was no production data yet, so this was a clean cut.

This plan covers the full spec (all 6 entities, the auth rewire, `AuthorizationService`, JWT/security config changes, seed data) within `security-access-control` only — no cross-service enforcement (gateway header propagation to other services) is in scope, matching how far this design was taken.

## Prior state (before this implementation)

- `User` (`sac_users`): `id, email(unique), password, fullName, role(UserRole), enabled, accountNonLocked, createdAt, updatedAt`.
- `AuthServiceImpl` implemented register/login/refresh/logout/validateToken/getCurrentUser against `IUserRepository`, keyed by email throughout.
- `UserDetailsServiceImpl` loaded `User` by email, built one `ROLE_xxx` `GrantedAuthority` from `UserRole`.
- `JwtTokenProvider`: access token subject = email, claim `role` = `UserRole.name()`; refresh token = random UUID in Redis (`refresh:{uuid} → email`); blacklist = `blacklist:{jti} → "true"`.
- `SecurityConfig`: stateless, CSRF off, public paths `/api/v1/auth/{register,login,refresh}` + `/actuator/**`, everything else `.authenticated()`.
- Gateway routed only `/api/v1/auth/**` to `security-access-control`.
- `pom.xml` already had everything needed: JJWT 0.12.6, MapStruct 1.5.5, Lombok, Redis, Spring Security, Validation — no new dependencies required.

## Package layout — `rbac` covers the whole service, not just the new entities

Since RBAC is the entire purpose of `security-access-control`, the `rbac` package segment applies to **everything** in the service — no split between "new stuff under `sac.rbac.*`" and "old auth stack under `sac.*`". Only the Spring Boot main class (`SecurityAccessControlApplication`) stays at the `com.himaloyit.buildnation.sac` root.

Everything else — new and pre-existing — lives under `com.himaloyit.buildnation.sac.rbac.*`:
`controllers/{rest,advice}`, `domain/{entities,dto,enums,mapper,model,repositories/iRepositories}`, `services/{iServices,impl}`, `security`, `config`, `util/exceptions`.

The existing auth classes were physically moved (package rename, not new code added alongside): `sac.security.*` → `sac.rbac.security.*`, `sac.services.*` → `sac.rbac.services.*`, `sac.controllers.*` → `sac.rbac.controllers.*`, `sac.domain.model.*` (`ApiResponse`, `RegisterRequest`, `LoginRequest`, `RefreshTokenRequest`, `AuthResponse`) → `sac.rbac.domain.model.*`, `sac.config.RedisConfig` → `sac.rbac.config.RedisConfig`, `sac.util.exceptions.InvalidTokenException` → `sac.rbac.util.exceptions.InvalidTokenException`, `sac.controllers.advice.GlobalResponseEntityException` → `sac.rbac.controllers.advice.GlobalResponseEntityException`.

## Entities

`Principal`, `Role`, `Resource`, `Permission` (ManyToOne → `Resource`), `PrincipalRole` (join, unique `(principal_id, role_id)`), `RolePermission` (join, unique `(role_id, permission_id)`) — fields per §1/§2 above. All six share a new `@MappedSuperclass` `RbacAuditableEntity` (`status`, `createdBy`, `createdDate`, `updatedBy`, `updatedDate`, `@Version version`) to avoid repeating the same six columns six times — `@SuperBuilder` used throughout instead of plain `@Builder` so the builder chains correctly through inheritance.

`@Version` optimistic locking + `createdBy`/`updatedBy` audit columns are a new pattern for this codebase (every other entity uses plain `createdAt`/`updatedAt` set manually) — intentional per the spec, scoped to the `rbac` package only.

**`createdBy`/`updatedBy` population**: a `CurrentPrincipalContext` helper (`sac.rbac.security`) reads `SecurityContextHolder.getContext().getAuthentication().getName()` — real caller identity (the authenticated principal's `principalCode`), falling back to `"system"` only when there's no authenticated caller (e.g. the startup seeder).

## Auth rewire: User → Principal

**Login identifier split** — `Principal.email` is only present for `principalType = USER`, but the JWT subject must be a universal identifier valid for every principal type:
- JWT subject = `principalCode`, always (never email).
- `UserDetailsServiceImpl.loadUserByUsername(String identifier)` does a dual lookup: `IPrincipalRepository.findByPrincipalCode(identifier)` first, falling back to `findByEmail(identifier)` — one method serves both `DaoAuthenticationProvider` during login (invoked with the typed email) and `JwtAuthenticationFilter` on every subsequent request (invoked with the `principalCode` pulled from the JWT subject).
- Builds `GrantedAuthority` from both roles (`ROLE_{roleCode}`) and permissions (`PERMISSION_{permissionCode}`) resolved fresh from `IAuthorizationService` on every call — this is the spec's "JwtAuthenticationConverter" requirement, implemented as an extension of the existing `UserDetailsServiceImpl` flow rather than the OAuth2-resource-server `JwtAuthenticationConverter` type (this project doesn't use that Spring Security module).

**`AuthServiceImpl`**: `register` stays USER-only (self-service signup only makes sense for humans; other principal types are admin-provisioned via `POST /api/v1/principals`). Builds `Principal` with `principalCode = email`, `credentialType=PASSWORD`, assigns the requested `roleCode` or the seeded `VIEWER` default via `IPrincipalRoleService.assignRole`. `login` authenticates via `AuthenticationManager`, updates `lastLoginAt`, and returns `AuthResponse` with `principal` (renamed from `user`) plus convenience `roles`/`permissions` string lists — resolved via `IAuthorizationService` for client UX, **not** embedded in the JWT itself (per §5 — DB-loaded authorities per request, no staleness).

`JwtTokenProvider.generateAccessToken(String principalCode)` — dropped the `role` claim/param entirely. `getEmailFromToken`/`getEmailFromRefreshToken` renamed to `getPrincipalCodeFromToken`/`getPrincipalCodeFromRefreshToken`.

Deleted outright: `User.java`, `UserRole.java`, `UserDTO.java`, `IUserRepository.java`, `IUserMapper.java`, `UserNotFoundException.java`, `UserAlreadyExistsException.java`. Replaced by `PrincipalDTO` and two generic exceptions reused across every entity in this package: `EntityNotFoundException` (404, mirrors the cdm pattern) and `DuplicateCodeException` (409).

## `IAuthorizationService`

```
boolean hasPermission(String principalCode, String permissionCode)
boolean hasAccess(String principalCode, String apiPath, HttpMethod httpMethod)
Set<String> getPermissionCodes(String principalCode)
Set<String> getRoleCodes(String principalCode)
```

Joins `Principal → PrincipalRole → Role → RolePermission → Permission (→ Resource)` via JPQL repository queries (`IRoleRepository.findActiveRolesByPrincipalCode`, `IPermissionRepository.findActivePermissionsByPrincipalCode` with `JOIN FETCH perm.resource`), filtered to `Status.ACTIVE` at every hop. `hasAccess` matches `resource.apiPath` against the request path via `AntPathMatcher` (same class `JwtAuthenticationFilter` already uses).

`getPermissionCodes`/`getRoleCodes` are `@Cacheable` (Redis, cache names `principal-permissions`/`principal-roles`, keyed by `principalCode`). `PrincipalRoleService.assignRole/revokeRole` evict both caches for the affected principal (via `CacheManager`, since the key is `principalCode` not a direct method arg); `RolePermissionService.assignPermission/revokePermission` evict `principal-permissions` for every `Principal` holding that `Role` (small fan-out query via `IPrincipalRoleRepository.findByRole_RoleId`).

## REST surface

Full CRUD for `Resource`, `Permission`, `Role`, `Principal` at `/api/v1/{resources,permissions,roles,principals}`, plus assignment sub-resources (`POST`/`DELETE /roles/{roleId}/permissions/{permissionId}`, `POST`/`DELETE /principals/{principalId}/roles/{roleId}`) and a read-only `AuthorizationController` at `/api/v1/authz` exposing `IAuthorizationService` directly (`/principals/{code}/permissions`, `/principals/{code}/roles`, `/check`, `/check-access`).

**Every write/read endpoint is gated with `@PreAuthorize("hasAuthority('PERMISSION_{entity}:{action}')")`** matching the seeded permission codes (`principal:create/view/update/delete`, same for `role`/`permission`/`resource`) — this was *not* in the original plan and had to be added after verification testing showed any authenticated principal could hit any endpoint regardless of role (see "Corrections made during verification" below).

## Seed data

`RbacSeeder` (`sac.rbac.config`, idempotent `CommandLineRunner`) seeds, only if absent:
- Roles: `ADMIN`, `MP_MINISTER`, `PROJECT_OFFICER`, `ENGINEER`, `FINANCE_OFFICER`, `VIEWER` (the actual BuildNation role list from the original spec, not the generic prompt's `CARD_*` banking example).
- Resources + Permissions: one pair per `{principal,role,permission,resource} × {create,view,update,delete}` (16 total), scoped to `security-access-control`'s own new admin API only — seeding for `member-management`/`cdm` endpoints is deferred until cross-service enforcement actually checks them.
- `RolePermission`: `ADMIN` → all 16; `VIEWER` → the 4 `*:view` permissions only.
- **A bootstrap `admin@buildnation.local` `Principal` with the `ADMIN` role** (configurable via `rbac.admin.email`/`rbac.admin.password`, default `Admin@123`) — added during verification once `@PreAuthorize` was wired in: without it, nobody could ever reach `ADMIN`, since assigning a role requires `principal:update`, which only `ADMIN` holds, and no `Principal` exists on a fresh database.

## Gateway wiring

Added `/api/v1/principals/**`, `/api/v1/roles/**`, `/api/v1/permissions/**`, `/api/v1/resources/**`, `/api/v1/authz/**` to the existing `security-access-control-route` path list in `gateway-service-config`.

## Corrections made during verification (not in the original plan)

End-to-end testing through the gateway surfaced three real gaps, all fixed:
1. **No `@PreAuthorize` anywhere** — the original plan specified permission codes and seeded them, but never wired `@PreAuthorize` onto the controllers, so authorization was effectively a no-op (any authenticated principal could do anything). Added to every write/read endpoint across all four CRUD controllers.
2. **`AccessDeniedException` was swallowed by the generic `Exception` handler**, returning 500 instead of 403, because Spring MVC's `@ExceptionHandler` resolution happens before Spring Security's `ExceptionTranslationFilter` would otherwise convert it. Added an explicit `@ExceptionHandler(AccessDeniedException.class)` → 403.
3. **Deleting a `Role`/`Permission`/`Resource` still referenced by a join row threw a raw `DataIntegrityViolationException`** → 500 with a full SQL stack trace in the response body. Added a generic handler → 409 with a friendly message, covering every entity's delete endpoint without needing bespoke reference-checking in each service.
4. **No bootstrap admin** — see seed data section above; without this, `@PreAuthorize` made the system permanently unusable (nobody could ever be granted `ADMIN`).

